#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:1
msgid "Feature Name: `unsafe_fields`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:2
msgid "Start Date: 2023-07-13"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:3
msgid ""
"RFC PR: [rust-lang/rfcs#3458](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3458)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:4
msgid ""
"Rust Issue: [rust-lang/rust#132922](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/132922)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:6
msgid "Summary"
msgstr "摘要"

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:8
msgid ""
"This RFC proposes extending Rust's tooling support for safety hygiene to "
"named fields that carry library safety invariants. Consequently, Rust "
"programmers will be able to use the `unsafe` keyword to denote when a named "
"field carries a library safety invariant; e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:14 src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:161
msgid ""
"/// # Safety\n"
"    ///\n"
"    /// `ptr` is a shared reference to a valid-but-unaligned instance of "
"`T`.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:22
msgid ""
"Rust will enforce that potentially-invalidating uses of such fields only "
"occur in the context of an `unsafe` block, and Clippy's "
"[`missing_safety_doc`](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-clippy/master/index."
"html#missing_safety_doc) lint will check that such fields have accompanying "
"safety documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:28
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr "動機"

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:30
msgid ""
"Safety hygiene is the practice of denoting and documenting where memory "
"safety obligations arise and where they are discharged. Rust provides some "
"tooling support for this practice. For example, if a function has safety "
"obligations that must be discharged by its callers, that function _should_ "
"be marked `unsafe` and documentation about its invariants _should_ be "
"provided (this is optionally enforced by Clippy via the "
"[`missing_safety_doc`](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-clippy/master/index."
"html#missing_safety_doc) lint). Consumers, then, _must_ use the `unsafe` "
"keyword to call it (this is enforced by rustc), and _should_ explain why its "
"safety obligations are discharged (again, optionally enforced by Clippy)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:38
msgid ""
"Functions are often marked `unsafe` because they concern the safety "
"invariants of fields. For example, [`Vec::set_len`](https://doc.rust-lang."
"org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.set_len) is `unsafe`, because it directly "
"manipulates its `Vec`'s length field, that carries the invariants that it is "
"less than the capacity of the `Vec` and that all elements in the `Vec<T>` "
"between 0 and `len` are valid `T`. It is critical that these invariants are "
"upheld; if they are violated, invoking most of `Vec`'s other methods will "
"induce undefined behavior."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:46
msgid ""
"To help ensure such invariants are upheld, programmers may apply safety "
"hygiene techniques to fields, denoting when they carry invariants and "
"documenting why their uses satisfy their invariants. For example, the "
"`zerocopy` crate maintains the policy that fields with safety invariants "
"have `# Safety` documentation, and that uses of those fields occur in the "
"lexical context of an `unsafe` block with a suitable `// SAFETY` comment."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:52
msgid ""
"Unfortunately, Rust does not yet provide tooling support for field safety "
"hygiene. Since the `unsafe` keyword cannot be applied to field definitions, "
"Rust cannot enforce that potentially-invalidating uses of fields occur in "
"the context of `unsafe` blocks, and Clippy cannot enforce that safety "
"comments are present either at definition or use sites. This RFC is "
"motivated by the benefits of closing this tooling gap."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:58
msgid "Benefit: Improving Field Safety Hygiene"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:60
msgid ""
"The absence of tooling support for field safety hygiene makes its practice "
"entirely a matter of programmer discipline, and, consequently, rare in the "
"Rust ecosystem. Field safety invariants within the standard library are "
"sparingly and inconsistently documented; for example, at the time of "
"writing, `Vec`'s capacity invariant is internally documented, but its length "
"invariant is not."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:65
msgid ""
"The practice of using `unsafe` blocks to denote dangerous uses of fields "
"with safety invariants is exceedingly rare, since Rust actively lints "
"against the practice with the `unused_unsafe` lint."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:68
msgid ""
"Alternatively, Rust's visibility mechanisms can be (ab)used to help enforce "
"that dangerous uses occur in `unsafe` blocks, by wrapping type definitions "
"in an enclosing `def` module that mediates construction and access through "
"`unsafe` functions; e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:73
msgid ""
"/// Used to mediate access to `UnalignedRef`'s conceptually-unsafe fields.\n"
"///\n"
"/// No additional items should be placed in this module. Impl's outside of "
"this module should\n"
"/// construct and destruct `UnalignedRef` solely through `from_raw` and "
"`into_raw`.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:79 src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:87
msgid ""
"/// # Safety\n"
"        ///\n"
"        /// `ptr` is a shared reference to a valid-but-unaligned instance of "
"`T`.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:103
msgid ""
"This technique poses significant linguistic friction and may be untenable "
"when split borrows are required. Consequently, this approach is uncommon in "
"the Rust ecosystem."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:106
msgid ""
"We hope that tooling that supports and rewards good field safety hygiene "
"will make the practice more common in the Rust ecosystem."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:109
msgid "Benefit: Improving Function Safety Hygiene"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:111
msgid ""
"Rust's safety tooling ensures that `unsafe` operations may only occur in the "
"lexical context of an `unsafe` block or `unsafe` function. When the safety "
"obligations of an operation cannot be discharged entirely prior to entering "
"the `unsafe` block, the surrounding function must, itself, be `unsafe`. This "
"tooling cue nudges programmers towards good function safety hygiene."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:116
msgid ""
"The absence of tooling for field safety hygiene undermines this cue. The "
"[`Vec::set_len`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html#method."
"set_len) method _must_ be marked `unsafe` because it delegates the "
"responsibility of maintaining `Vec`'s safety invariants to its callers. "
"However, the implementation of [`Vec::set_len`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/"
"std/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.set_len) does not contain any explicitly "
"`unsafe` operations. Consequently, there is no tooling cue that suggests "
"this function should be unsafe — doing so is entirely a matter of programmer "
"discipline."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:122
msgid ""
"Providing tooling support for field safety hygiene will close this gap in "
"the tooling for function safety hygiene."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:125
msgid "Benefit: Making Unsafe Rust Easier to Audit"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:127
msgid ""
"As a consequence of improving function and field safety hygiene, the process "
"of auditing internally `unsafe` abstractions will be made easier in at least "
"two ways. First, as previously discussed, we anticipate that tooling support "
"for field safety hygiene will encourage programmers to document when their "
"fields carry safety invariants."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:132
msgid ""
"Second, we anticipate that good field safety hygiene will narrow the scope "
"of safety audits. Currently, to evaluate the soundness of an `unsafe` block, "
"it is not enough for reviewers to _only_ examine `unsafe` code; the "
"invariants upon which `unsafe` code depends may also be violated in safe "
"code. If `unsafe` code depends on field safety invariants, those invariants "
"can currently be violated in any safe (or unsafe) context in which those "
"fields are visible. So long as Rust permits safety invariants to be violated "
"at-a-distance in safe code, audits of unsafe code must necessarily consider "
"distant safe code. (See [_The Scope of Unsafe_](https://www.ralfj.de/"
"blog/2016/01/09/the-scope-of-unsafe.html).)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:142
msgid ""
"For crates that practice good safety hygiene, reviewers will mostly be able "
"to limit their review of distant routines to only `unsafe` code."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:145
msgid "Guide-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:147
msgid ""
"A safety invariant is any boolean statement about the computer at a time "
"_t_, which should remain true or else undefined behavior may arise. Language "
"safety invariants are imposed by Rust itself and must never be violated; e."
"g., a `NonZeroU8` must _never_ be 0."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:151
msgid ""
"Library safety invariants, by contrast, are imposed by an API. For example, "
"`str` encapsulates valid UTF-8 bytes, and much of its API assumes this to be "
"true. This invariant may be temporarily violated, so long as no code that "
"assumes this safety invariant holds is invoked."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:155
msgid ""
"Safety hygiene is the practice of denoting and documenting where memory "
"safety obligations arise and where they are discharged. To denote that a "
"field carries a library safety invariant, use the `unsafe` keyword in its "
"declaration and document its invariant; e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:169
msgid ""
"You should use the `unsafe` keyword on any field that carries a library "
"safety invariant that differs from the invariant provided by its type."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:172
msgid ""
"The `unsafe` field modifier is only applicable to named fields. You should "
"avoid attaching library safety invariants to unnamed fields."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:175
msgid ""
"Rust provides tooling to help you maintain good field safety hygiene. "
"Clippy's [`missing_safety_doc`](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-clippy/"
"master/index.html#missing_safety_doc) lint checks that `unsafe` fields have "
"accompanying safety documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:178
msgid ""
"The Rust compiler enforces that uses of `unsafe` fields that could violate "
"its invariant — i.e., initializations, writes, references, and copies — must "
"occur within the context of an `unsafe` block. For example, compiling this "
"program:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:186
msgid "/// SAFETY: `pow` must be between 0 and 29 (inclusive).\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:203
msgid ""
"/// # Safety\n"
"    ///\n"
"    /// The caller promises to not write a value greater than 29 into the "
"returned reference.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:212
msgid "...emits the errors:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:214
msgid ""
"```\n"
"error[E0133]: initializing type with an unsafe field is unsafe and requires "
"unsafe block\n"
"  --> src/lib.rs:14:14\n"
"   |\n"
"14 |         Some(Self { pow })\n"
"   |              ^^^^^^^^^^^^ initialization of struct with unsafe field\n"
"   |\n"
"   = note: unsafe fields may carry library invariants\n"
"\n"
"error[E0133]: use of unsafe field is unsafe and requires unsafe block\n"
"  --> src/lib.rs:18:9\n"
"   |\n"
"18 |         self.pow\n"
"   |         ^^^^^^^^ use of unsafe field\n"
"   |\n"
"   = note: unsafe fields may carry library invariants\n"
"\n"
"error[E0133]: use of unsafe field is unsafe and requires unsafe block\n"
"  --> src/lib.rs:25:14\n"
"   |\n"
"25 |         &mut self.pow\n"
"   |              ^^^^^^^^ use of unsafe field\n"
"   |\n"
"   = note: for more information, see <https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/"
"edition-guide/rust-2024/unsafe-op-in-unsafe-fn.html>\n"
"   = note: unsafe fields may carry library invariants\n"
"note: an unsafe function restricts its caller, but its body is safe by "
"default\n"
"  --> src/lib.rs:24:5\n"
"   |\n"
"24 |     pub unsafe fn as_mut_log(&mut self) -> &mut u8 {\n"
"   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n"
"note: the lint level is defined here\n"
"  --> src/lib.rs:1:38\n"
"   |\n"
"1  | #![forbid(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)]\n"
"   |           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n"
"\n"
"For more information about this error, try `rustc --explain E0133`.\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:253
msgid ""
"...which may be resolved by wrapping the use-sites in `unsafe { ... }` "
"blocks; e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:290
msgid ""
"You may use `unsafe` to denote that a type relaxes its type's library safety "
"invariant; e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:294
msgid ""
"/// SAFETY: `maybe_invalid` may not contain valid UTF-8. Nonetheless, it "
"MUST always contain\n"
"    /// initialized bytes (per language safety invariant on `str`).\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:300
msgid ""
"...but you _must_ ensure that the field is soundly droppable before it is "
"dropped. A `str` is bound by the library safety invariant that it contains "
"valid UTF-8, but because it is trivially destructible, no special action "
"needs to be taken to ensure it is in a safe-to-drop state."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:304
msgid ""
"By contrast, `Box` has a nontrivial destructor that requires that its "
"referent has the same size and alignment that the referent was allocated "
"with. Merely adding the `unsafe` modifier to a `Box` field, e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:310
msgid "/// SAFETY: `data`'s logical type has `type_id`.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:312
msgid "/// SAFETY: See [`BoxErased::data`].\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:318
msgid "// cast `Box<T>` to `Box<[MaybeUninit<u8>]>`\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:320
msgid "// SAFETY: …\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:331
msgid ""
"...does not ensure that using `BoxedErased` or its `data` field in safe "
"contexts cannot lead to undefined behavior: namely, if `BoxedErased` or its "
"`data` field is dropped, its destructor may induce UB."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:334
msgid ""
"In such situations, you may avert the potential for undefined behavior by "
"wrapping the problematic field in `ManuallyDrop`; e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:347
msgid "When _Not_ To Use Unsafe Fields"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:349
msgid "Relaxing a Language Invariant"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:351
msgid ""
"The `unsafe` modifier is appropriate only for denoting _library_ safety "
"invariants. It has no impact on _language_ safety invariants, which must "
"_never_ be violated. This, for example, is an unsound API:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:357
msgid ""
"// SAFETY: The value of `zeroed` consists only of bytes initialized to `0`.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:368
msgid "...because `Zeroed::<NonZeroU8>::zeroed()` induces undefined behavior."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:370
msgid "Denoting a Correctness Invariant"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:372
msgid ""
"A library _correctness_ invariant is an invariant imposed by an API whose "
"violation must not result in undefined behavior. In the below example, "
"unsafe code may rely on `alignment_pow`s invariant, but not `size`'s "
"invariant:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:378
msgid ""
"/// The size of a type.\n"
"    ///\n"
"    /// # Invariants\n"
"    ///\n"
"    /// For well-formed layouts, this value is less than `isize::MAX` and is "
"a multiple of the alignment.\n"
"    /// To accommodate incomplete layouts (i.e., those missing trailing "
"padding), this is not a safety invariant.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:385
msgid ""
"/// The log₂(alignment) of a type.\n"
"    ///\n"
"    /// # Safety\n"
"    ///\n"
"    /// `alignment_pow` must be between 0 and 29.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:394
msgid ""
"The `unsafe` modifier should only be used on fields with _safety_ "
"invariants, not merely correctness invariants."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:397
msgid ""
"We might also imagine a variant of the above example where `alignment_pow`, "
"like `size`, doesn't carry a safety invariant. Ultimately, whether or not it "
"makes sense for a field to be `unsafe` is a function of programmer "
"preference and API requirements."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:401
msgid "Complete Example"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:403
msgid ""
"The below example demonstrates how field safety support can be applied to "
"build a practical abstraction with small safety boundaries ([playground]"
"(https://play.rust-lang.org/?"
"version=nightly&mode=debug&edition=2024&gist=e8aa2af933f5bf4892d1be951062538d)):"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:419
msgid ""
"/// An `Arc` that provides exclusive access to its referent.\n"
"///\n"
"/// A `UniqueArc` may have any number of `KeepAlive` handles which ensure "
"that\n"
"/// the inner value is not dropped. These handles only control dropping, and "
"do\n"
"/// not provide read or write access to the value.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:426
msgid ""
"/// # Safety\n"
"    ///\n"
"    /// While this `UniqueArc` exists, the value pointed by this `arc` may "
"not\n"
"    /// be accessed (read or written) other than via this `UniqueArc`.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:432 src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:640
msgid ""
"/// Keeps the parent [`UniqueArc`] alive without providing read or write "
"access\n"
"/// to its value.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:436 src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:643
msgid ""
"/// # Safety\n"
"    ///\n"
"    /// `T` may not be accessed (read or written) via this `Arc`.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:444
msgid "/// Constructs a new `UniqueArc` from a value.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:447
msgid ""
"// SAFETY: Since we have just created `arc` and have neither cloned it\n"
"        // nor leaked a reference to it, we can be sure `T` cannot be read "
"or\n"
"        // accessed other than via this particular `arc`.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:453
msgid ""
"/// Releases ownership of the enclosed value.\n"
"    ///\n"
"    /// Returns `None` if any `KeepAlive`s were created but not destroyed.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:457
msgid ""
"// SAFETY: Moving `arc` out of `Self` releases it from its safety\n"
"        // invariant.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:463
msgid ""
"/// Produces a `KeepAlive` handle, which defers the destruction\n"
"    /// of the enclosed value.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:466
msgid ""
"// SAFETY: By invariant on `KeepAlive::arc`, this clone will never be\n"
"        // used for accessing `T`, as required by `UniqueArc::arc`. The one\n"
"        // exception is that, if a `KeepAlive` is the last reference to be\n"
"        // dropped, then it will drop the inner `T`. However, if this "
"happens,\n"
"        // it means that the `UniqueArc` has already been dropped, and so "
"its\n"
"        // invariant will not be violated.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:484 src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:503
msgid ""
"// SAFETY: We do not create any other owning references to `arc` - we\n"
"        // only dereference it below, but do not clone it.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:488
msgid ""
"// SAFETY: We satisfy all requirements for pointer-to-reference\n"
"        // conversions [1]:\n"
"        // - By invariant on `&UnsafeCell<T>`, `ptr` is well-aligned, non-"
"null,\n"
"        //   dereferenceable, and points to a valid `T`.\n"
"        // - By invariant on `Self::arc`, no other `Arc` references exist "
"to\n"
"        //   this value which will be used for reading or writing. Thus, we\n"
"        //   satisfy the aliasing invariant of `&` references.\n"
"        //\n"
"        // [1] https://doc.rust-lang.org/1.85.0/std/ptr/index.html#pointer-"
"to-reference-conversion\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:507
msgid ""
"// SAFETY: We satisfy all requirements for pointer-to-reference\n"
"        // conversions [1]:\n"
"        // - By invariant on `&mut UnsafeCell<T>`, `ptr` is well-aligned,\n"
"        //   non-null, dereferenceable, and points to a valid `T`.\n"
"        // - By invariant on `Self::arc`, no other `Arc` references exist "
"to\n"
"        //   this value which will be used for reading or writing. Thus, we\n"
"        //   satisfy the aliasing invariant of `&mut` references.\n"
"        //\n"
"        // [1] https://doc.rust-lang.org/1.85.0/std/ptr/index.html#pointer-"
"to-reference-conversion\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:521
msgid "Reference-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:523
msgid "Syntax"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:525
msgid ""
"The [`StructField` syntax](https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/reference/items/"
"structs.html#structs), used for the named fields of structs, enums, and "
"unions, shall be updated to accommodate an optional `unsafe` keyword just "
"before the field `IDENTIFIER`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:538
msgid ""
"The use of unsafe fields on unions shall remain forbidden while the [impact "
"of this feature on unions](#safe-unions) is decided."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:541
msgid "Semantics"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:543
msgid ""
"Projections of fields marked `unsafe` must occur within the context of "
"`unsafe`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:545
msgid ""
"Clippy's [`missing_safety_doc`](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-clippy/"
"master/index.html#missing_safety_doc) lint ensures such fields have "
"accompanying safety documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:547
msgid "Rationale and Alternatives"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:549
msgid "The design of this proposal is primarily guided by three tenets:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:551
msgid ""
"[**Unsafe Fields Denote Safety Invariants**](#tenet-unsafe-fields-denote-"
"safety-invariants) A field _should_ be marked `unsafe` if it carries "
"arbitrary library safety invariants with respect to its enclosing type."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:554
msgid ""
"[**Unsafe Usage is Always Unsafe**](#tenet-unsafe-usage-is-always-unsafe) "
"Uses of `unsafe` fields that could violate their invariants _must_ occur in "
"the scope of an `unsafe` block."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:557
msgid ""
"[**Safe Usage is Usually Safe**](#tenet-safe-usage-is-usually-safe) Uses of "
"`unsafe` fields that cannot violate their invariants _should not_ require an "
"unsafe block."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:561
msgid ""
"This RFC prioritizes the first two tenets before the third. We believe that "
"the benefits of doing so — broader utility, more consistent tooling, and a "
"simplified safety hygiene story — outweigh its cost, [alarm fatigue](#alarm-"
"fatigue). The third tenet implores us to weigh this cost."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:565
msgid "Tenet: Unsafe Fields Denote Safety Invariants"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:567
msgid ""
"A field _should_ be marked `unsafe` if it carries library safety invariants."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:569
msgid ""
"We adopt this tenet because it is consistent with the purpose of the "
"`unsafe` keyword in other declaration positions, where it signals to "
"consumers of the `unsafe` item that their use is conditional on upholding "
"safety invariants; for example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:573
msgid ""
"An `unsafe` trait denotes that it carries safety invariants that must be "
"upheld by implementors."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:574
msgid ""
"An `unsafe` function denotes that it carries safety invariants that must be "
"upheld by callers."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:576
msgid "Tenet: Unsafe Usage is Always Unsafe"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:578
msgid ""
"Uses of `unsafe` fields that could violate their invariants _must_ occur in "
"the scope of an `unsafe` block."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:581
msgid ""
"We adopt this tenet because it is consistent with the requirements the "
"`unsafe` keyword imposes when applied to other declarations; for example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:584
msgid "An `unsafe` trait may only be implemented with an `unsafe impl`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:585
msgid ""
"An `unsafe` function is only callable in the scope of an `unsafe` block."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:587
msgid "Tenet: Safe Usage is Usually Safe"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:589
msgid ""
"Uses of `unsafe` fields that cannot violate their invariants _should not_ "
"require an unsafe block."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:591
msgid ""
"Good safety hygiene is a social contract and adherence to that contract will "
"depend on the user experience of practicing it. We adopt this tenet as a "
"forcing function between designs that satisfy our first two tenets. All else "
"being equal, we give priority to designs that minimize the needless use of "
"`unsafe`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:596
msgid "Alternatives"
msgstr "替代方案"

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:598
msgid ""
"These tenets effectively constrain the design space of tooling for field "
"safety hygiene; the alternatives we have considered conflict with one or "
"more of these tenets."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:601
msgid "Unsafe Variants"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:603
msgid ""
"We propose that the `unsafe` keyword be applicable on a per-field basis. "
"Alternatively, we can imagine it being applied on a per-constructor basis; e."
"g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:607 src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:616
msgid "// SAFETY: ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:621
msgid ""
"For structs and enum variants with multiple unsafe fields, this alternative "
"has a syntactic advantage: the `unsafe` keyword need only be typed once per "
"enum variant or struct with safety invariant."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:625
msgid ""
"However, in structs and enum variants with mixed safe and unsafe fields, "
"this alternative denies programmers a mechanism for distinguishing between "
"conceptually safe and unsafe fields. Consequently, any safety tooling built "
"upon this mechanism must presume that _all_ fields of such variants are "
"conceptually unsafe, requiring the programmer to use `unsafe` even for the "
"consumption of 'safe' fields. This violates [_Tenet: Safe Usage is Usually "
"Safe_](#tenet-safe-usage-is-usually-safe)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:632
msgid "Field Moving is Safe"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:634
msgid ""
"We propose that all uses of `unsafe` fields require `unsafe`, including "
"reading. Alternatively, we might consider making reads safe. However, a "
"field may carry an invariant that would be violated by a read. In the "
"[_Complete Example_](#complete-example), `KeepAlive<T>::arc` is marked "
"`unsafe` because it carries such an invariant:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:650
msgid ""
"Allowing `arc` to be safely moved out of `KeepAlive<T>` would create the "
"false impression that it is safe to use `arc` — it is not. By requiring "
"`unsafe` to read `arc`, Rust's safety tooling ensures a narrow safety "
"boundary: the user is forced to justify their actions when accessing `arc` "
"(which documents its safety conditions as they relate to `KeepAlive`), "
"rather than in downstream interactions with `UnsafeCell<T>` (whose methods "
"necessarily provide only general guidance). Consequently, we require that "
"moving unsafe fields out of their enclosing type requires `unsafe`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:657
msgid "Field Copying is Safe"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:659
msgid ""
"We propose that all uses of unsafe fields require `unsafe`, including "
"copying. Alternatively, we might consider making field copies safe. However, "
"a field may carry an invariant that could be violated as a consequence of a "
"copy. For example, consider a field of type `&'static RefCell<T>` that "
"imposes an invariant on the value of `T`. In this alternative proposal, such "
"a field could be safely copiable out of its enclosing type, then safely "
"mutated via the API of `RefCell`. Consequently, we require that copying "
"unsafe fields out of their enclosing type requires `unsafe`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:666
msgid "Copy Is Safe To Implement"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:668
msgid ""
"We propose that `Copy` is conditionally unsafe to implement; i.e., that the "
"`unsafe` modifier is required to implement `Copy` for types that have unsafe "
"fields. Alternatively, we can imagine permitting retaining Rust's present "
"behavior that `Copy` is unconditionally safe to implement for all types; e."
"g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:675
msgid ""
"/// # Safety\n"
"    ///\n"
"    /// `ptr` is an exclusive reference to a valid-but-unaligned instance of "
"`T`.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:691
msgid ""
"However, the `ptr` field introduces a declaration-site safety obligation "
"that is not discharged with `unsafe` at any use site; this violates "
"[**Tenet: Unsafe Usage is Always Unsafe**](#tenet-unsafe-usage-is-always-"
"unsafe)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:695
msgid "Nontrivial Destructors are Prohibited"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:697
msgid ""
"If a programmer applies the `unsafe` modifier to a field with a nontrivial "
"destructor and relaxes its invariant beyond that required by the field's "
"destructor, Rust cannot prevent the unsound use of that field in safe "
"contexts. This is, seemingly, a soft violation of [**Tenet: Unsafe Usage is "
"Always Unsafe**](#tenet-unsafe-usage-is-always-unsafe). We resolve this by "
"documenting that such fields are a serious violation of good safety hygiene, "
"and accept the risk that this documentation is ignored. This risk is "
"minimized by prevalence: we feel that relaxing a field's invariant beyond "
"that of its destructor is a rare subset of the cases in which a field "
"carries a relaxed invariant, which is itself a rare subset of the cases in "
"which a field carries a safety invariant."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:707
msgid ""
"Alternatively, we previously considered that this risk might be averted by "
"requiring that `unsafe` fields have trivial destructors, à la union fields, "
"by requiring that `unsafe` field types be either `Copy` or `ManuallyDrop`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:711
msgid ""
"Unfortunately, we discovered that adopting this approach would contradict "
"our design tenets and place library authors in an impossible dilemma. To "
"illustrate, let's say a library author currently provides an API of this "
"shape:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:718 src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:737
msgid "// SAFETY: [some additive invariant]\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:723
msgid "...and a downstream user currently consumes this API like so:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:730
msgid ""
"Then, `unsafe` fields are stabilized and the library author attempts to "
"refactor their crate to use them. They mark `unsafe_field` as `unsafe` and — "
"dutifully following the advice of a rustc diagnostic — wrap the field in "
"`ManuallyDrop`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:742
msgid ""
"But, to avoid a memory leak, they must also now provide a `Drop` impl; e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:747
msgid ""
"// SAFETY: `unsafe_field` is in a library-valid\n"
"        // state for its type.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:754
msgid ""
"This is a SemVer-breaking change. If the library author goes though with "
"this, the aforementioned downstream code will no longer compile. In this "
"scenario, the library author cannot use `unsafe` to denote that this field "
"carries a safety invariant; this is _both_ a hard violation of [**Tenet: "
"Unsafe Fields Denote Safety Invariants**](#tenet-unsafe-fields-denote-safety-"
"invariants), and (in requiring trivially `unsafe` drop glue), a violation of "
"[**Tenet: Safe Usage is Usually Safe**](#tenet-safe-usage-is-usually-safe)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:761
msgid "Unsafe Wrapper Type"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:763
msgid ""
"This RFC proposes extending the Rust language with first-class support for "
"field (un)safety. Alternatively, we could attempt to achieve the same "
"effects by leveraging Rust's existing visibility and safety affordances. At "
"first blush, this seems plausible; it's trivial to define a wrapper that "
"only provides unsafe initialization and access to its value:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:798
msgid ""
"However, this falls short of the assurances provided by first-class support "
"for field safety. The safety conditions of its accessors inherit the safety "
"conditions of the field that the `Unsafe` was read or referenced from. "
"Consequently, what safety proofs one must write when using such a wrapper "
"depend on the dataflow of the program."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:803
msgid ""
"And worse, certain dangerous flows do not require `unsafe` at all. For "
"instance, unsafe fields of the same type can be laundered between fields "
"with different invariants; safe code could exchange `Even` and `Odd`s' "
"`val`s:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:817
msgid ""
"We can plug this particular hole by adding a type parameter to `Unsafe` that "
"encodes the type of the outer datatype, `O`; e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:825
msgid ""
"However, it remains possible to exchange unsafe fields within the same type; "
"for example, safe code can freely exchange the values of `len` and `cap` of "
"this hypothetical vector:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:836
msgid ""
"The [`unsafe-fields`](https://crates.io/crates/unsafe-fields) crate plugs "
"this hole by extending `Unsafe` with a const generic that holds a hash of "
"the field name. Even so, it remains possible for safe code to exchange the "
"same unsafe field between different instances of the same type (e.g., "
"exchanging the `len`s of two instances of the aforementioned `Vec`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:841
msgid "These challenges motivate first-class support for field safety tooling."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:843
msgid "More Syntactic Granularity"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:845
msgid ""
"This RFC proposes the rule that _a field marked `unsafe` is unsafe to use_. "
"This rule is flexible enough to handle arbitrary field invariants, but — in "
"some scenarios — requires that the user write trivial safety comments. For "
"example, in some scenarios, an unsafe field is trivially sound to read:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:851
msgid ""
"/// # Safety\n"
"    ///\n"
"    /// `val` is an even number.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:859
msgid ""
"// SAFETY: Reading this `val` cannot\n"
"        // violate its invariant.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:866
msgid ""
"In other scenarios, an unsafe field is trivially sound to `&`\\-reference "
"(but not `&mut`\\-reference)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:868
msgid ""
"Since it is impossible for the compiler to precisely determine the safety "
"requirements of an unsafe field from a type-directed analysis, we must "
"_either_ choose a usage rule that fits all scenarios (i.e., the approach "
"adopted by this RFC) _or_ provide the user with a mechanism to signal their "
"requirements to the compiler. Here, we explore this alternative."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:873
msgid ""
"The design space of syntactic knobs is vast. For instance, we could require "
"that the user enumerate the operations that require `unsafe`; e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:876
msgid "`unsafe(init,&mut,&,read)` (everything is unsafe)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:877
msgid "`unsafe(init,&mut,&)` (everything except reading unsafe)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:878
msgid ""
"`unsafe(init,&mut)` (everything except reading and `&`\\-referencing unsafe)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:879 src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:887
msgid "etc."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:881
msgid ""
"Besides the unclear semantics of an unparameterized `unsafe()`, this design "
"has the disadvantage that the most permissive (and thus dangerous) semantics "
"are the cheapest to type. To mitigate this, we might instead imagine "
"reversing the polarity of the modifier:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:885
msgid "`safe(read)` all operations except reading are safe"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:886
msgid ""
"`safe(read,&)` all operations except reading and `&`\\-referencing are safe"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:889
msgid ""
"...but using `safe` to denote the presence of a safety invariant is probably "
"too surprising in the context of Rust's existing safety tooling."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:892
msgid ""
"Alternatively, if we are confident that a hierarchy of operations exists, "
"the brevity of the API can be improved by having the presence of one "
"modifier imply others (e.g., `unsafe(&mut)` could denote that "
"initialization, mutation and `&mut`\\-referencing) are unsafe. However, this "
"requires that the user internalize this hierarchy, or else risk selecting "
"the wrong modifier for their invariant."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:897
msgid ""
"Although we cannot explore the entire design space of syntactic modifiers "
"here, we broadly feel that their additional complexity exceeds that of our "
"proposed design. Our proposed rule that _a field marked `unsafe` is unsafe "
"to use_ is both pedagogically simple and failsafe; i.e., so long as a field "
"is marked `unsafe`, it cannot be misused in such a way that its invariant is "
"violated in safe code."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:903
msgid "Mixing Syntactic Knobs with a Wrapper Type"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:905
msgid ""
"One alternative proposed in this RFC's discussion recommends a combination "
"of syntactic knobs and a wrapper type. In brief, a simple [`Unsafe` wrapper "
"type](#unsafe-wrapper-type) would be provided, along with two field safety "
"modifiers:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:909
msgid "`unsafe` All uses except reading are `unsafe`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:911
msgid ""
"`unsafe(mut)` All uses except reading and `&`\\-referencing are `unsafe`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:914
msgid ""
"Under this proposal, a programmer would use some combination of `unsafe`, "
"`unsafe(mut)` and `Unsafe` to precisely tune Rust's safety tooling "
"protections, depending on the hazards of their invariant."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:917
msgid ""
"The primary advantage of this approach is that it results in comparatively "
"fewer instances in which [the programmer must write a 'trivial' safety proof]"
"(#trivial-safety-proofs). However, it achieves this by front-loading the "
"requirement that the programmer imagine all possible safety hazards of their "
"field. A mistake, here, may lead to a false sense of security if Rust fails "
"to require `unsafe` for uses that are, in fact, dangerous. By contrast, this "
"RFC requires that programmers resolve these questions only on an as-needed "
"basis; e.g., until you need to `&`\\-reference a field, you do not need to "
"confront whether doing so is _always_ a safe operation."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:925
msgid ""
"This alternative also inherits some of the disadvantages of [`Unsafe` "
"wrapper types](#unsafe-wrapper-type); namely that the safety proofs needed "
"to operate on an `Unsafe` wrapper value depend on the dataflow of the "
"program; the wrapper value must be traced to its originating field so that "
"field's safety documentation may be examined."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:930
msgid ""
"Comparatively, we believe that this RFC's proposal is both pedagogically "
"simpler and less prone to misuse, and that these benefits outweigh its "
"[drawbacks](#drawbacks)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:933
msgid "Drawbacks"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:935
msgid "Trivial Safety Proofs"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:937
msgid ""
"The primary drawback of this proposal is that it — in some scenarios — "
"necessitates writing 'trivial' safety proofs. For example, merely reading "
"`Vec`'s `len` field obviously cannot invalidate its invariant; nonetheless, "
"this field, if marked `unsafe`, would be `unsafe` to read. An `unsafe` block "
"and attendant `SAFETY` comment is required. In most cases, this is a one-"
"time chore: the maintainer can define a _safe_ accessor (i.e., [`Vec::len`]"
"(https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.len)) that "
"encapsulates this proof. However, in cases where multiple, partial field "
"borrows are required, such an accessor cannot be invoked. [Future language "
"extensions that permit partial borrows may resolve this drawback.](#partial-"
"borrows)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:947
msgid ""
"At the extreme, a programmer frustrated with field safety tooling might opt "
"to continue with the status quo approach for maintaining field invariants. "
"Such rebuttals of safety tooling are not unprecedented in the Rust "
"ecosystem. Even among prominent projects, it is not rare to find a "
"conceptually unsafe function or impl that is not marked unsafe. The "
"discovery of such functions by the broader Rust community has, occasionally, "
"provoked controversy."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:953
msgid ""
"This RFC takes care not to fuel such flames; e.g., [**Tenet: Unsafe Fields "
"Denote Safety Invariants**](#tenet-unsafe-fields-denote-safety-invariants) "
"admonishes that programmers _should_ — but **not** _must_ — denote field "
"safety invariants with the `unsafe` keyword. It is neither a soundness nor "
"security issue to continue to adhere to the current convention of using "
"visibility to enforce field safety invariants."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:959
msgid "Prior art"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:961
msgid ""
"Some items in the Rust standard library have "
"`#[rustc_layout_scalar_valid_range_start]`, "
"`#[rustc_layout_scalar_valid_range_end]`, or both. These items have "
"identical behavior to that of unsafe fields described here. It is likely "
"(though not required by this RFC) that these items will be required to use "
"unsafe fields, which would reduce special-casing of the standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:966
msgid "Unresolved questions"
msgstr "未解決的問題"

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:968
msgid ""
"If the syntax for restrictions does not change, what is the ordering of "
"keywords on a field that is both unsafe and mut-restricted?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:971
msgid "Terminology"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:973
msgid ""
"This RFC defines three terms of art: _safety invariant_, _library safety "
"invariant_, and _language safety invariant_. The meanings of these terms are "
"not original to this RFC, and the question of which terms should be assigned "
"to these meanings [is being hotly debated](https://github.com/rust-lang/"
"unsafe-code-guidelines/issues/539). This RFC does not prescribe its "
"terminology. Documentation of the unsafe fields tooling should reflect "
"broader consensus, once that consensus is reached."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:980
msgid "Future possibilities"
msgstr "未來的可能性"

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:982
msgid "Partial Borrows"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:984
msgid ""
"The primary drawback of this proposal is that it — in some scenarios — "
"necessitates writing 'trivial' safety proofs. For example, merely reading "
"`Vec`'s `len` field obviously cannot invalidate its invariant; nonetheless, "
"this field, if marked `unsafe`, would be `unsafe` to read. An `unsafe` block "
"and attendant `SAFETY` comment is required. In most cases, this is a one-"
"time chore: the maintainer can define a _safe_ accessor (i.e., [`Vec::len`]"
"(https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.len)) that "
"encapsulates this proof. However, in cases where multiple, partial field "
"borrows are required, such an accessor cannot be invoked. Future language "
"extensions that permit partial borrows will resolve this drawback."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:993
msgid "Syntactic Knobs and Wrapper Types"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:995
msgid ""
"While we are confident that this RFC has the best tradeoffs among the "
"alternatives in the design space, it is not a one-way door. Changes to the "
"default semantics of `unsafe` could be realized over an edition boundary. "
"This RFC is also forward-compatible with some future additions of some "
"[combinations](#mixing-syntactic-knobs-with-a-wrapper-type) of [syntactic "
"knobs](#more-syntactic-granularity) and [wrapper types](#unsafe-wrapper-"
"type). For example, in addition to this RFC's `unsafe` modifier, additional "
"variants in the form `unsafe(<modifiers>)` (e.g., `unsafe(mut)`) could be "
"added to denote that some subset of uses is always safe."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:1003
msgid "Safe Unions"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:1005
msgid ""
"Today, unions provide language support for fields with subtractive "
"_language_ invariants. Unions may be safely defined, constructed and mutated "
"— but require unsafe to read. Consequently, it is possible to place a union "
"into a state where its fields cannot be soundly read, using only safe code; "
"e.g. ([playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?"
"version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2021&gist=1d816399559950ccae810c4a41fab4e9)):"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:1022
msgid "// Now, neither `tricky.a` nor `tricky.b` are in a valid state.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:1026
msgid ""
"The possibility of such unions makes it tricky to retrofit a mechanism for "
"safe access: Because unsafe was not required to define or mutate this union, "
"the invariant that makes reading sound is entirely implicit."
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:1030
msgid ""
"Speculatively, it might be possible to make the subtractive language "
"invariant of union fields _explicit_; e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3458-unsafe-fields.md:1040
msgid ""
"Migrating today's implicitly-unsafe unions to tomorrow's explicitly-unsafe "
"unions over an edition boundary would free up the syntactic space for safe "
"unions."
msgstr ""
